import json
S = []
H1 = lambda t: S.append(["HEADING_1", t]); P = lambda t: S.append(["NORMAL_TEXT", t]); B = lambda t: S.append(["BULLET", t])
P("Status: done 2026-10-26.")
H1("Question")
P("CoT-Control's strict graders score a trace 0 after a single violation, so most models sit at 0 and the scale cannot show partial attempts, meta-discussion, or a constraint moved into the answer. Can a cheap judge label those reliably, and what do the labels show on Neel's 34-model run and on our 17 gpt-oss-120b checkpoints?")
H1("Methods")
B("Rubric with 9 categories (complied, mostly complied, complied then broke, broke then complied, token attempt, noticed but no attempt, answer only, no attempt, degenerate), a share-complied score, first-break position, meta-discussion and whether the answer applies the rule. Reference labels: GPT-6 Astra (Codex, high effort) and Claude subagents labelling the same blinded rows; consensus = rows where both agree.")
B("Three labelled sets: 200 rows (100 Neel, 100 ours), a fresh 200 with frozen decision rules written first, and 100 rows from the families where the judge failed (Qwen 3.5, Kimi K2.5/K2.6, Inkling, Gemma 4, GLM-4.5).")
B("Judges: TypeSafe Jev 1.13 through OpenRouter's /api/alpha/decisions endpoint (label probabilities, $0.042/M input tokens), gpt-oss-120b, and 8 cheap OpenRouter models with the rubric verbatim. Final system (v8): the reference grader decides complied vs not on the reasoning; a failing reasoning with an answer that passes the grader is 'displaced to the answer'; the judge labels only the rest.")
B("Code and data: zeus ~/code/results/neel-astra/labels (RUBRIC.md, v8.py, answer_grader.py, judges_eval.py, scale/labels_v8.jsonl).")
H1("Results")
B("Astra vs Claude category agreement: 80.5% (kappa 0.77) on set 1, 82.5% on set 2, 60% on set 3. Most set-3 disagreement is one unlabelled pattern: unconstrained working with drafts of a constrained answer inside the reasoning (Astra: token attempt; Claude: answer only).")
B("Jev alone: meta-discussion AUC 0.985 to 0.995, full compliance AUC 0.97 to 0.98, but 9-way category only 53 to 57% on consensus rows. It confuses 'mostly complied' with 'complied' and drafts of the answer with compliance.")
B("v8 hybrid with Jev: 72.0% / 72.1% / 70.0% 9-way on sets 1 / 2 / 3. The reference grader on the answer text identifies displacement exactly (63 true positives, 0 false positives on set 3).")
B("Best cheap judges in the v8 frame (9-way on sets 1 / 2 / 3; failures): DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 80.1 / 83.6 / 85.0, no failures, $0.47 per 500 rows; MiMo v2.6 Flash 78.2 / 81.1 / 84.2, 16 empty replies, $0.19; Claude Haiku 5.5 86.4 / 81.3 / 78.9, 27 content-filter refusals, $0.29; Jev 72 / 72 / 70, $0.05; gpt-oss-120b 61 / 58 / 65, $0.10.")
B("At scale (Jev v8 over 34,247 traces, $4.32): displacement to the answer is the main failure mode for Qwen 3.5 397B (81.5%), Kimi K2.5 (73.1%), Kimi K2.6 (70.4%), Inkling (62.0%), and near 0 for our gpt-oss checkpoints. CoT-control-trained checkpoints almost never mention the constraint (manytypes 0.9%, grpo3 2.3% vs base 22.8%). Against NCRI across Neel's 34 models: meta-discussion +0.50 (p=0.003); complied +0.12; complied or partial +0.12; displaced -0.22.")
H1("Caveats and next steps")
B("The partial categories still over-credit unconstrained traces as 'mostly complied' (the main remaining error), so per-model partial rates are upper bounds. Re-run the scale labelling with DeepSeek V4.1 Flash in the v8 frame (about $32) once the rubric decides how drafts of the answer count.")
json.dump(S, open("spec_judge.json", "w"))
